WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.194 --> 00:00:02.783
(latin music)

00:00:02.783 --> 00:00:04.570
- You can walk into my office

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.332
with any type of case,

00:00:06.332 --> 00:00:09.583
and I can handle it, no matter what it is.

00:00:09.583 --> 00:00:11.000
We don't give up.

00:00:18.252 --> 00:00:19.985
- [Narrator] This is Jay J. Armes,

00:00:19.985 --> 00:00:22.702
and he's maybe the world's most
famous private investigator.

00:00:22.702 --> 00:00:24.174
- I don't say that I am.

00:00:24.174 --> 00:00:25.837
People say that I am.

00:00:25.837 --> 00:00:27.308
- [Narrator] Back in 1958,

00:00:27.308 --> 00:00:28.893
Armes founded The Investigators.

00:00:28.893 --> 00:00:30.648
- [Jay] The Investigators is a company

00:00:30.648 --> 00:00:33.178
that was established by Jay Armes

00:00:33.178 --> 00:00:35.631
to conduct every type of investigation.

00:00:35.631 --> 00:00:38.664
We cover the whole spectrum.

00:00:38.664 --> 00:00:39.497
(cool music)

00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:41.176
- [Narrator] And like all
legendary heroic figures,

00:00:41.176 --> 00:00:43.398
Jay has triumphed over tragedy.

00:00:43.398 --> 00:00:45.219
- When I was 11 years old,

00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:47.392
a friend came over to my home,

00:00:47.392 --> 00:00:49.585
had a box of dynamite caps,

00:00:49.585 --> 00:00:53.227
they exploded, had blown
my hands off at the wrist.

00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:54.060
- [Narrator] So young Jay was outfitted

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.814
with prosthetics, and he managed to

00:00:55.814 --> 00:00:56.647
turn a
- turn a negative

00:00:56.647 --> 00:00:57.654
into a positive.

00:00:57.654 --> 00:00:58.551
- [Narrator] Exactly.

00:00:58.551 --> 00:00:59.440
(soft, quick music)

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.559
Here's Jay J. Armes petting his
white Siberian Tiger, Simba.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:05.483
And here he is with his
53-year-old pet chimp, Gypsy.

00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:07.705
And here's Gypsy with Jay's son, Jay.

00:01:07.705 --> 00:01:09.795
So Jay, how many cases have you solved?

00:01:09.795 --> 00:01:11.545
- Hundreds of cases,

00:01:11.545 --> 00:01:12.554
all over the world.

00:01:12.554 --> 00:01:13.912
- [Narrator] Can you be a
little bit more specific?

00:01:13.912 --> 00:01:15.381
- [Jay] You ask an artist,

00:01:15.381 --> 00:01:17.271
how many paintings have you done?

00:01:17.271 --> 00:01:21.441
He can't tell you, I've done
20 paintings or 200 paintings.

00:01:21.441 --> 00:01:23.727
I take my craft like an artist.

00:01:23.727 --> 00:01:26.884
- [Narrator] And he's had
some pretty famous clientele.

00:01:26.884 --> 00:01:27.889
(cool music)
- [Jay] Most famous case

00:01:27.889 --> 00:01:29.530
was Marlon Brando.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:33.909
He says, Jay, somebody has
kidnapped my son Christian.

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:36.828
He gave me the information where
the boy was kidnapped from.

00:01:36.828 --> 00:01:37.913
- [Narrator] Legend has
it that Jay J. Armes

00:01:37.913 --> 00:01:40.344
tracked down Christian
to the jungles of Mexico,

00:01:40.344 --> 00:01:42.682
flying his helicopter and
staying up for five days

00:01:42.682 --> 00:01:44.151
without food or water,

00:01:44.151 --> 00:01:46.540
until he came upon a camp
with eight armed men.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.186
- I heard him coughing in a little tent.

00:01:49.186 --> 00:01:51.072
I went into the little tent,

00:01:51.072 --> 00:01:52.909
and there was Christian Brando.

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:54.310
He didn't know me.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.091
He sees this guy with two hooks,

00:01:56.091 --> 00:01:58.297
so he thought I was one of the bad guys.

00:01:58.297 --> 00:01:59.385
I said, no, no.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.799
Your father hired me.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:02.632
(soft guitar music)

00:02:02.632 --> 00:02:04.722
- [Narrator] For Jay J. Armes,
what was once an impediment

00:02:04.722 --> 00:02:05.814
has become an asset,

00:02:05.814 --> 00:02:07.879
and has led to a long, fruitful career.

00:02:07.879 --> 00:02:09.097
- [Jay] I been doin' this type of work

00:02:09.097 --> 00:02:11.113
for over 45 years.

00:02:11.113 --> 00:02:12.109
- [Narrator] How old are you, Jay?

00:02:12.109 --> 00:02:14.122
- Oh, I'm 'bout 150.

00:02:14.122 --> 00:02:15.417
- [Narrator] At 150,

00:02:15.417 --> 00:02:16.945
- But I'm really not 150.

00:02:16.945 --> 00:02:18.851
- [Narrator] At not 150, it's safe to say

00:02:18.851 --> 00:02:21.127
most of Jay J. Armes'
adventures are behind him.

00:02:21.127 --> 00:02:23.062
But he's not slowin' down any time soon.

00:02:23.062 --> 00:02:26.064
- [Jay] No, I have more
work now than ever.

00:02:26.064 --> 00:02:28.129
- [Narrator] Jay, do you
see yourself as a legend?

00:02:28.129 --> 00:02:29.229
How do you wanna be remembered?

00:02:29.229 --> 00:02:33.681
- On my obituary, "Jay
Armes did it his way."

00:02:33.681 --> 00:02:36.542
One-liner, and that's it.

00:02:36.542 --> 00:02:39.792
(lilting guitar music)

